Subject: COSMO: Holographic Universe Theory

	 >No.  The core of QM requires no faith.  It is a model for predicting
	 >observable reality.  Testing it only requires that it reliably predict
	 >observable reality, not that it explain that reality (predictions come
	 >before the test, explanations come after).

		Correct me if I'm wrong (and boy, is that an invitation), but my 
	 understanding is that the beginnings of QM were attempts to explain an 
	 inability to predict *observable* reality at the quantum level.Ergo, 
	 information gathered from other experiments testing other predictions 
	 entirely produced anomolies that were *explained* by a theory that could 
	 then be tested by predicting an outcome, performing an experiment to test 
	 the theory and seeing if the prediction was correct.
		Which came first, the theory or the prediction? 

QM doesn't explain anything; it merely predicts things better.  As I
learn more QM, I can better predict the world, but that doesn't
capture any sense of what's really going on beyond the math.  The
interpretations might be looked at as attempted explanations (what do
you 'mean' by superposition?).

All those anomolies could also be *explained* as gremlins mucking with
your results in order to preserve the mystery of the world.  That
explanation doesn't help in dealing with the world because it doesn't
help you predict it in the future (gremlins will do something
different in the future).

		It was Clarke, and the original quote (unavailable at the moment) 
	 mentioned primitive people. Something along the lines of "To a primitive 
	 society, any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable 
	 from magic." I might add that to many many modern technological society 
	 citizens, technology is akin to magic. And what are UFOs but saints and 
	 devils in modern garb (at least according to the National Enquirer).

Thank you.  You've restored my faith :-)  I usually don't hear the
qualifier.  I still don't know why Clarke was brought into this :-)

		Actually, I prefer the Many Universes model, but that's probably because I 
	 can visualize it better.

Robin: Check me on this please:
One of the confusions people have about the Many-Universes
interpretation is that they are independent universes.  I think the
way the math works out the 'universes' tend to mostly be the same
because it's really just one universe with superpositions.  That means
that a particle in superposition (thus a source of a univese fork) is
still a single particle, and so interacting with it in one 'universe'
affects it in the other.  Thus gravity/space-time tends to be stable
across all these parallel 'universes'.

dean

