"It's like killing your children so you can get pregnant again."

Sci.astro (and probably other groups) has an interesting thread on
NASA's dictum that the Galileo program cut its budget (again). The
programs cut may gut the whole reason for going to Jupiter.
Especially serious would be dropping funding for upgrades that would
allow the defunct High Gain Antenna, the one that failed to unfurl
properly, to be by bypassed or compensated for.

How could this happen?

It seems that NASA depends on vendor support and lobbying--the
aerospace lobby. And since Galileo is _already built_ and no
additional contract money will come out of it (a bit, but not much),
the mood is developing to just let it die. Not to kill it off
officially--one never does that--but just to keep it on a minimal
level of life support. Meanwhile, start more space probes to keep
McDonnell-Douglas, Rockwell, Boeing, and the other contractors happy,
keep their campaign contributions rolling in, and extend the day of
reckoning into the next millenium. Everyone gets a political payoff.

One unnamed NASA administrator calls this the "kill your children so
you can get pregnant again" strategy.

This whole open-loop operation is the reason to just kill off all of
NASA. Run by slave labor (our taxes) for reasons nobody can explain
(the Space Station does virtually nothing that SkyLab--remember
that?--did nearly 20 years ago), NASA is a boat anchor.

Make work. Leaning on shovels. "Never time to do it right, always time
to do it twice." Time to put a bullet through its brain.

(Sorry, Robin and Amara, but it's time for you to find honest work.
Robin is going off to Caltech, so we're almost there. As for Dani, Nick, and
all the other space enthusiasts, fine, if it's privately financed and
run. If it's worth doing, it's worth doing for a profit. And the
contrapositive of that, too!)

The truly exciting science I've seen recently is being done on the
Keck Telescope, built largely with private funds and producing better
results than the Hobble for a fraction of the price.

KILL THE SPACE STATION!
KILL THE SUPERCONDUCTING SUPERCOLLIDER!
KILL THE HUMAN GENOME PROJECT!

..and stop the growing talk of a "National Nanotechnology Initiative."
(Drexler seethes at me at parties when I dispute his notion of a
NTI..he claims that "this time it'll be different," cites the reported
Japanese state efforts, and claims the payoffs are simply too high to
*not* be pursuing this. Needless to say, I think he's dead wrong, for
a variety of reasons I've mentioned here over the past year.)

Stop funding pet projects with slave labor.

(Sorry for a rant, but when I heard the growing consensus to let
Galileo die of budget cuts, only so a new boondoggle can be started, I
realized this epitomizes the open-loop, zero accountability nature of
all such non-market, political patronage efforts. It's all a sham.
Even the SuperCollider is being sold as a "jobs program"...wanna bet
that once it's finished and all those construction workers go back on
the dole, it'll begin to die the death of a thousand budget cuts?)

