Let me add some comments about various proposed "missions" for the
Space Station:

* astronomical - a dead issue, except for occasional solar studies, as
the vibration caused by astronauts moving around is unacceptable
(there was once a proposal that they'd be forced to take sleep periods
in cocoons to minimize vibration...this flopped for a lot of reasons).

* microgravity research -- easier to do in small satellites, and a
whole lot cheaper, too.
     
("What about growing high-purity silicon? It'll help Americans remain
competitive with the Japanese!" Well, it isn't needed, it won't work,
and no American companies, be they crystal producers or silicon
consumers, has the slightest interest in this. My last boss at Intel,
head of Components Research, Dr. John Carruthers, was formerly NASA's
chief scientist (or one of them...). He admitted to me in 1986 that
the whole "crystal in space" plan was cooked up to get Reagan to sign
on for the Space Station.)

* SDI -- a dead issue, and a fixed-orbit space station is too easy a
target for nearly any weapon.

So what's left? I may have forgotten something that was purported to
be a reason to build the space station (and I'm leaving out obviously
nonsensical reasons like "a way station for going back to the moon").
but I doubt any viable reasons remain.

Asteroid mining may be one sort of reason to eventually move into
space in a big way, and maybe Dani Eder is right that Boeing could do
it (perhaps) for a few tens of billions. If so, it'll get done,
eventually.

Enough for now, as this was supposed to be just a correction.

-Tim


