It's not even universal in Europeans --- not even in the Celto-Teuton blue-eye
types like me you'd expect the adaptation to be best-established in.  (I presume
everybody here knows that the whole blue eyes/blond hair/pale skin thing is
a set of recent adaptations for foggy/cloudy/misty climates with low sunlight
levels?)

I have a friend who's another one of those nasty melanin-deficient "Ice People"
Leonard Jeffries raves about, ancestry much like mine (French/German/Scots/
Irish).  She certainly ought to have the lactose-eating mutation, but nope. 
She had chronic migraines, digestive problems and insomnia for years before
she twigged that she might be lactose-intolerant, had herself tested, and
dropped dairy products from her diet.  Her health problems disappeared.

She's a bright lady.  Did research.  Diagnosed herself correctly.

Friends, when they test American populations at random they find 10-20%
lactose intolerance.  (At least, that's what she told me.)  Millions of
people are having chronic health problems brought on by the dairy products
they eat and drink.  On the evidence known to me, this is a *far* more
serious problem than meat-eating.

Although I've never been tested, I suspect I'm borderline lactose-intolerant
myself.  I've disliked milk since my mid-teens, can't stand yogurt, and am
*extremely* averse to cheese.  Although I don't think that's mono-causal; I
also seem to have an aversion, bordering on allergy, to fermentation products
(especially alcohol), and aged cheeses are the ones that make me feel ill
(I can handle cream cheese, cottage cheese, and Rondelet).

Unlike the world's evangelical Vegans, I don't feel any drive to persuade
others that my dietary quirks are the nutritional ideal.  But my experience
and my friend's research suggest that sub-clinical chronic allergy to dairy
products may be a *major* undiagnosed health problem.
