 Being a vegetarian does not assure perfect cholesterol levels or 
 normal HDL/LDL ratios. In fact, the high potassium content found in 
 the average vegetarian diet is one reason why *total* cholesterol 
 levels may be actually higher with vegetarians than with those on a 
 mixed diet, as potassium can be a factor in stimulating the liver to 
 produce more cholesterol (whith some people blaming genetics for it).

 The HDL/LDL cholesterol ratio is dependent on the intake ratio of
 sodium/phosphorus to the calcium/magnesium content of your food, or
 any supplements which affect that ratio, as well as the dietary re-
 duction of processed/heated/hydrogenated oils/fats (margarine), pork, 
 processed nuts, dairy products and - stress!

 I see "out of control" cholesterol cases every day that don't respond
 to their physician's regimen of diet and medication, and after a few
 weeks on sodium/phosphorus-raising and calcium/magnesium-lowering
 supplements, cholesterol readings quickly become normal, or even
 better than normal, as reflected by my own *and* their physician's
 measurements. In many instances, I have to recommend increasing the 
 intake of eggs for unrelated reasons, which reflects *favorably* on 
 subsequent retests, while B-Complex, if taken, has to be discontinued.

M(>  Why are my triglycerides normal?......]

 Since serum triglyceride measurements at this time are as inaccurate
 as cholesterol tests were prior to the advent of HDL/LDL fractions,
 (not that cholesterol tests are that accurate now), I wouldn't waste 
 my time with them, unless you can find someone that is not only able 
 to measure total levels, but also the simple and complex fractions as 
 well. (I use an electronic test that measures both separately).

 Triglycerides can be generally kept within normal, as long as one's
 calcium intake isn't too high or protein/phosphorus intake too low, 
 and as long as sugar, sweets, honey, etc., are kept to a minimum.
 Progesterone and zinc supplements tend to raise *total* triglycerides 
