From: p199-ac@garnet.berkeley.edu (Zak Zaidman)
Subject: Singing v. Milk

Tim Starr here.  My account's residence is down, so I'm contributing from this
one.

The reason why I quit drinking milk in order to sing better is that this is a
widespread custom amongst singers.  Milk induces a minor allergic reaction in
just about everyone, and it also provides the precursors for phlegm, snot, the
stuff that clogs up your nose and throat.  It also includes a neurotransmitter
I seem to be a bit deficient in (serotonin, I think), so I used to drink a
lot of milk - without knowing what I liked so much about it.

In retrospect I see that it made be sneeze and cough needlessly.  I can recall
having milk and cereal for breakfast then running off to school, only to become
unable to keep running due to respiratory congestion - about 15-20 minutes
after the milk entered my system.  Last year I was up in Davis, CA, and it
was incredibly dusty.  We had pizza for dinner, and the cheese provided
precursors for nasal congestion that lasted the whole night for me!

I have allergies to things like cat and horse hair, timothy grass, etc., and
these are all exacerbated if I drink milk, almost negligible if I don't.  At
least the symptoms of respiratory congestion don't appear - my nose and
throat stay clear, even if I do sneeze and cough a little and my eyes itch.
Usually washing suffices to end the sneezing and itching, and I could never
do that when I still drank milk.

Cheese doesn't seem to have those effects on me nearly as much, but I don't
eat that much of it, either.

Perry says that eating protein depletes your calcium in digestion; since milk
contains both protein and calcium, do they cancel each other out?  Are they
in the right proportions?

As for Perry's claim that milk is best for calves, the milk that is produced
for human consumption differs from that for calves, although I don't recall
in what ways.  I just know from my dairy farmer relatives that when a cow is
pregnant and nursing that their milk is different and not put into the vats
along with the rest of the stuff headed off to the processing plants to
become homogenized, pasteurized, and all that.  I also know from drinking
their product before it's been so processed (they keep it pure by stirring, i
think) that it tastes much better!  Fresh milk is much tastier than anything
you get at your local grocery store.

I'm not much a purist about staying away from dairy products.  I just don't
consume much of them.  Cheese is about the most common thing I eat, ice
cream and milk the rarest.  I do eat whipped cream occasionally.  This is
part of my general nonchalant way of having a healthy diet.  I know from
when I lived in college dorms that I have a much better diet than most of
my peers, but I've never put much effort into it.  I probably do eat too much
meat, but I like it.  My likes can't be too bad, though: when one of my
dorms had a "health food night," I thought it was the best they'd ever made,
and wished they'd serve food like that every night!

Tim Starr

